---
import Markdown from '../layouts/Markdown.astro'
import type { PageMeta } from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import { SITE, personRef } from '../data/schema'
import { easternDate, formatDate, isoDate } from '../data/dates'

// Bump this whenever the way AI is used on the site changes
const updated = easternDate('2026-11-13') as Date

const meta: PageMeta = {
  title: 'AI Disclosure',
  description: 'How I use AI on this site, from grabbing app screenshots to drafting blog posts and writing structured data, and what always stays in my hands.',
  schema: {
    breadcrumbs: [{ name: 'AI Disclosure', url: `${SITE}/ai-disclosure` }],
    page: {
      dateModified: isoDate(updated),
      author: personRef,
    },
  },
}
---

<Markdown meta={meta}>
  <span slot="eyebrow">Last updated <time datetime={isoDate(updated)}>{formatDate(updated)}</time></span>

  <p>
    I use AI to help run this site. I think you deserve to know where it shows up, so this page lays out
    exactly what it does, what it doesn't, and how I keep an eye on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The short version: AI handles the tedious work so I can spend my time on the parts that matter. Every
    opinion, recommendation, and project on this site is mine, and I review everything AI produces before it
    goes live.
  </p>

  <h2>Screenshots and assets</h2>
  <p>
    The screenshots of my iPhone apps, like the ones on the <a href="/repeaters">Repeaters</a> and <a href="/intervals">Intervals</a> pages, are captured by AI. It boots up the iOS Simulator, taps through
    each screen, and grabs the shots I ask for.
  </p>
  <p>
    These are real screenshots of the real apps. AI is doing the clicking, not the drawing, so what you see is
    what you get when you download them.
  </p>

  <h2>Blog posts</h2>
  <p>
    Every post on <a href="/blog">my blog</a> starts as an outline I write myself: the topic, the points I want
    to make, and the opinions I want to share. AI turns that outline into a rough first draft so I'm not
    staring at a blank page.
  </p>
  <p>
    From there, I rewrite the draft by hand. I change the wording to sound like me, cut what doesn't belong, and
    add the stories and details only I would know. By the time a post is published, it has been hand-authored
    over from top to bottom, and the ideas in it were always mine to begin with.
  </p>

  <h2>SEO and AEO</h2>
  <p>
    AI helps with the behind-the-scenes information that search engines and AI assistants read to understand
    this site. That includes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Page titles and the short descriptions you see in search results</li>
    <li>JSON-LD structured data that describes my pages, posts, apps, and projects</li>
    <li>Alt text for images and social sharing cards</li>
    <li>The <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> file that gives language models a plain-text map of the site</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    I review all of it before it ships to make sure it accurately describes the page it belongs to.
  </p>

  <h2>Code</h2>
  <p>
    I also use AI as a coding assistant while building and maintaining this site. I treat it the way I describe in <a href="/blog/ai-in-development-worst-mentor-best-mentee">AI in Development: the Worst Mentor, the Best Mentee</a>:
    I make the decisions and give it specific instructions, then I read and understand every change before it
    gets merged.
  </p>

  <h2>What stays human</h2>
  <p>
    Some things I will always do myself. My opinions and recommendations come from my own experience. The
    projects in <a href="/projects">my portfolio</a> are work I actually did. When you reach out through <a href="/contact">my contact form</a>, the reply comes from me.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions?</h2>
  <p>
    If you have questions about how I use AI, or you think something on the site isn't living up to this page, <a href="/contact">let me know</a>. I'll update this page whenever the way I use AI changes.
  </p>
</Markdown>
